pleased to say we're joined now bye  nadeem zahari the minister  responsible for the vaccine rollout mr  sahab we good to have you on the program  this morning and  my goodness there is a huge amount of  pressure on your shoulders  isn't there um in essence it looks like  we need to be moving from  300 000 vaccinations a week to 300 000  vaccinations a day now even by hercules  famously exacting standards that is a  herculean task in front of you  it is and it is a big task uh the nhs  both in england scotland wales and  northern ireland  have got a very clear delivery plan and  the plan is  as we launched initially with pfizer a  novel vaccine in hospital hubs then into  what are called primary care networks so  a network of five or six  gps coming together one leads and then  they begin vaccinations that has grown  massively that's the 730  the bulk of that delivery network now  we then go to national vaccination hubs  that the nhs have got planned and of  course the community pharmacies and the  independent  pharmacy sector as well so it's a  massive  effort uh by nhs england and england nhs  scotland wales and northern ireland  and they have a very clear plan they've  delivered 1.3  million over 1.3 million uh doses  already a quarter of those so one in  four  uh 80 year olds uh have already had the  first  jab the vaccination and in a couple of  weeks time that those  you know 25 of 80 year olds  will be protected and of course will  then get their second job as well so it  is a  herculean effort um i'm pleased to see  kirstama saying that we need to come  together i hope we get the support  of all political parties all members of  parliament  and the whole country as we're seeing  volunteers coming through the national  volunteering service  and thank you to the sun newspaper for  their campaign  and of course to send john's ambulance  who are leading on the volunteering and  nato's professionals as well let's just  talk  about about the achievability of that  which the government has set  out we heard from the prime minister the  ambition is of course to get you know  nine on 14 million people vaccinated by  the middle of next month but by the end  of this month we're looking at around  about 2 million  vaccinations every week um we heard  chris whittie say that this was  realistic but not easy matt hancock  suggesting  this is a best case scenario i mean how  would you categorize it  doable at a stretch  it's a big target and i think the prime  minister's right to  uh set us challenging targets uh we need  to  get on with this and do it as quickly  but as safely as possible which is why  again i commend the nhs for the plan  that they've put  in place the military are embedded in  the  in the team and i pay great tribute to  brigadier phil prosser  of 101 logistics brigade who absolutely  is  there focused on this delivery program  so it is a coming together  of you know the nation to deliver this  it is a stretching target no doubt  very stretching target but i'm confident  that you know  with this plan that the nhs have put  together we will deliver this  sure i i hate to keep hammering the  point but there have been occasions  throughout this pandemic where the  government has perhaps over-promised and  under-delivered taking the 14 million by  the middle of next month and 2 million a  week look  if we are getting two million  vaccinations a week by the end of this  month let's just  gently suggest that that would be a  total of eight million  charitably by the end of the month that  means  six million doses in two weeks now it's  early in the morning my mathematical  skills are pretty rudimentary  but you know six million divided by two  weeks is  three million vaccinations a week two  million a week  in and of itself if we hit that we're  not going to hit the target next month  isn't that  analysis accurate well i'm confident  that as we begin to deploy and  get more sites uh operational  i talked about the hospitals the gps of  course the community pharmacies and the  national vaccination centers  so we will be at over a thousand  sites vaccinating i'm confident you will  see  uh certainly from the 4th of january to  the 11th of january  a real step up so we've done  over 1.3 million uh since the 8th of  december you're going to see a massive  acceleration  of that but we need to do more than 2  million doses a week don't we  to get to that 14 million target in the  middle of next month  yeah no absolutely and you're going to  see the the that increase the nhs have  got a very clear plan  uh we've got a fantastic team uh working  seven days a week all hours to deliver  this  and as i said i think it's a stretching  target  no doubt it is a stretching target but i  think it's one that we should absolutely  uh uh look to deliver because if we do  deliver that and deliver the  those one to four categories that the  joint committee on vaccination and  immunization  have uh identified are the most  vulnerable then  you will hopefully see that uh you know  a couple of weeks later reflected  in the the coming down  of the hospitalization rates and  ultimately mortality begins  to also be impacted so we see less  people dying from this  horrible virus just in terms of the  the problems that there may well be at  the moment one that has been identified  and  referenced by the prime minister  yesterday was that was the batch testing  of the virus once the the fit and fill  section of the  the production process has taken place  it is taking as long as perhaps 20  days for batches to receive that  approval  how can we trim that down but remain  safe so so there are a couple of things  so  first of all when the bulk vaccine goes  to the fill and finish  operation then it does need to  effectively go through a number of tests  including something called the sterility  test  now different batches are in different  timelines for that  the mhra and i again pay tribute to june  rain and her team  are doing everything in their capability  to do it  properly without cutting corners and  safely  to test then every batch because the  worst thing we can do  is uh in a national vaccination program  that is the biggest in this nation's  history  is to get this wrong and to have you  know a vaccine that is  not effective effectively being used so  it is important but we do look at each  and every step to make sure  that it is done as efficiently as  possible  and as we get more volume coming through  from the manufacturers  uh you i'm confident that we've got  enough visibility to be able to really  go after that prime minister's target  of uh mid-february being able to deliver  you know north of uh 14 million  uh doses and offer those to those first  one to four categories  just in terms of the the the assets that  we're using to get the vaccine into  people's arms there are some  uh within the pharmaceutical within the  the pharmacy industry i should say  rather than the pharmaceutical industry  those working in pharmacies  there appear to be thousands of people  there who of course are  experienced in the administration of  vaccines because of the the influenza  vaccine  why are they not already being stood up  to being used it does seem like  there is a resource lying there waiting  to be taken up with the government  there is a very important resource and  we are taking it up  and we're working with the uh community  pharmacy  sector very important sector and of  course the independence as you  mentioned as well what we did was stand  up the hospitals first because as chris  whittie has quite rightly  uh uh identified when you have a novel  vaccine you want to make sure  that as you you know increase the  numbers of people being vaccinated you  do it  in a careful way where you can observe  people the regulator has a what is  called the yellow card system so that we  see  uh how people are uh you know reacting  to the vaccine  in a very safe way and then we go into  gps  gps  are the most effective way of getting  into say for example  the care homes it's very important in  those  that first category in fact is to get  into care home residence and that's the  best way to get into care homes  so we went with the gps which is the nhs  plan i was talking about  then international vaccination centers  which you will see uh  uh imminently and then uh into the  uh community pharmacies and the  independent sector so every sector  will play a part in this and i commend  both the independent pharmacies and the  community pharmacies for stepping up and  coming forward  and as i said we're working with them to  operationalize them and you'll see that  uh coming through in the nhs plan  a number of people will have been  concerned to hear that the chief medical  officer  suggesting yesterday that you know  christmas time  this year there could still be some  restrictions in place face masks  social distancing what is your view on  all of that  what is the likelihood uh that we will  still have some restrictions i mean the  vaccine  we know is not the uh not the be all and  end all  it is not the only response we all still  have to play our part won't we  of course and and my focus is very much  on vaccine deployment  it is the largest vaccination program in  the history of this country  i think once we begin to make a  difference to those  most vulnerable people so categories one  to four by mid  february we go then all through the the  rest of the categories down to category  nine um but do you still see  restrictions being in place  at christmas uh of the potential for  restrictions being in place at christmas  as  chris whittie said yesterday well i  think as chris whittie has also said is  is  this will be a a political decision and  when we get to that point of inflection  between where community transmission  is really impacted by the vaccination  program  and of course by uh continuing  to ramp up the testing uh infrastructure  in the united kingdom  then the the decision for  government for us is to say well you  know when is it  right to begin to lift some of the  non-pharmaceutical interventions  that we're having to make like the  current uh lockdown that we are  in uh that will be there will come a  moment when we see  where we can basically uh manage this  virus  uh and be able to bring it back under  control  and that is a a decision for the  government  nadeem sahabi good to see you this  morning a busy man indeed thanks for  joining us  thank you

